  "id": "COMPOUND:24771 - PROTEIN:6323",
  "verbalization": "Haliclorensin B is implicated in the regulation of DNA polymerase iota through a multi-step mechanism involving the vacuolar ATP synthase subunit S1. This pathway is supported by three distinct evidence chains, all of which link Haliclorensin B to DNA polymerase iota via intermediate targets. The first evidence chain connects Haliclorensin B to vacuolar ATP synthase subunit S1, which is then targeted by 5,6-Dimethoxy-2-(3,4-dimethoxyphenyl)-1h-quinolin-4-one, a compound that also targets DNA polymerase iota. The second evidence chain shows Haliclorensin B directly targeting vacuolar ATP synthase subunit S1, which is also a target of levonorgestrel, another compound that acts on DNA polymerase iota. The third evidence chain provides a more complex pathway, where Haliclorensin B targets vacuolar ATP synthase subunit S1, which is then targeted by a structurally diverse compound, ultimately leading to DNA polymerase iota. These findings suggest that Haliclorensin B may exert its effects on DNA polymerase iota through the modulation of vacuolar ATP synthase subunit S1, potentially by disrupting energy metabolism or cellular signaling pathways. The consistent involvement of vacuolar ATP synthase subunit S1 across different evidence chains supports a core mechanism, though the exact molecular interactions remain to be fully elucidated.",
